WHY THIS WAS ABANDONED
-----------------------
This patch (both the version below and a v2 that added handle.Finish()
before every eviction, to close a suspected use-after-eviction race) passed
every standalone MIOpen-only repro thrown at it -- including a 200-iteration
repeat of the identical shape, and a repro mixing MIOPEN_REDUCE_TENSOR_ADD/
MAX/MIN calls across many shapes on one handle to mimic ORT's real usage
pattern -- 0 failures, every time. It nonetheless failed ORT's real test
suite (onnxruntime_test_all --gtest_filter=*ReductionOpTest*)
non-deterministically: sometimes clean, sometimes
ReductionOpTest.ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large returns small integer sequences
(1, 2, 3, 4...) instead of sums -- a WORSE, less-understood failure
signature than the original bug's numerically-off-but-plausible-looking
wrong sums. No standalone repro, however elaborate, reproduced this ORT-only
failure, which means there was no fast, reliable way left to iterate on a
fix -- every test cycle meant a multi-minute, sometimes-inconclusive full
ORT suite run. Rather than ship a patch that trades one characterized bug
for a worse, uncharacterized one, this was abandoned and ReduceSum was left
on the unpatched baseline. See gfx803/KERNEL_BUGS.md, "The ReduceSum
kernel-cache mystery", for the full investigation.

MIOpen (pinned tag rocm-6.4.4): after every miopenReduceTensor() dynamic-CK
dispatch, evict that call's own kernel-cache and program-cache entries from
the Handle, forcing the next call that would have reused them to
recompile+reload from scratch instead.

WHY (and why this is NOT a clean fix)
--------------------------------------
This does not identify or fix the actual defect. It works around a real,
reproducible, gfx803-specific correctness bug in MIOpen's dynamic-reduction
kernel dispatch by making that dispatch avoid ever reusing a cached compiled
kernel across shapes on the same handle -- the one condition every
experiment below found eliminates the bug, without ever pinning down why.

THE BUG: miopenReduceTensor's dynamic-reduction path launches two kernels
per call (a "prepare" kernel that writes a tensor descriptor into a scratch
workspace, and a "main" kernel that reads it back and does the reduction).
A standalone, ORT-independent MIOpen-only repro -- one miopenHandle_t, a
sweep of ReduceTensor ADD calls over many (m,n) shapes, matching
ReductionOpTest.ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large's exact call pattern -- shows
about 15% of shapes in the sweep return wrong sums. The same shapes always
pass 100% of the time when run alone (fresh handle, nothing else compiled
or cached yet). Reversing the shape iteration order changes WHICH shapes
fail and makes it worse (16/52 wrong instead of 8/52) -- proof this is
about accumulated handle state, not a property of any individual shape.

EVERY ONE of the following was tested and ruled out as the mechanism, each
with a targeted, isolated experiment (not just "seemed to not matter"):
  - GPU dispatch-order race between separate kernel launches:
    AMD_SERIALIZE_KERNEL=3 (forces fully synchronous kernel execution) has
    zero effect -- same shapes fail, same mismatch counts.
  - Workspace visibility between the prepare and main kernel specifically:
    an earlier attempt at this exact patch inserted handle.Finish() between
    the two dispatches (confirmed active via HIP API tracing -- an explicit
    hipEventSynchronize genuinely occurs) with zero effect.
  - MIOpen's on-disk JIT kernel-binary cache: identical failures whether
    that disk cache starts cold or warm.
  - GPU device-memory address reuse / cache coherency on a freed-then-
    reallocated address: identical failures even with hipMalloc replaced by
    one static, never-freed buffer pool for the entire sweep.
  - Kernel launch occupancy: adding __launch_bounds__(BlockSize, 1) to both
    the prepare and main kernels (forcing minBlocksPerMultiprocessor=1) has
    zero effect.
  - Host-side memory corruption in reducetensor.cpp's own C++ logic:
    running the repro under valgrind --track-origins=yes reproduces the
    exact same failures with ZERO memcheck errors reported.
  - Insufficiently-specific compile-time cache keys (two different tunables
    colliding on the same compiled binary): ruled out by inspection --
    get_definition_string_from_tunable/get_network_config_string_from_tunable
    both fully encode BlockSize/GredThreadBufferLength/etc, so different
    tunables never share a cache key.

What DOES reliably eliminate every failure, confirmed repeatedly:
  - A genuinely fresh miopenHandle_t for every single call (nothing has
    ever been compiled/cached on it before) -- not usable as a real fix,
    since ORT and PyTorch both hold one handle for a whole session/stream,
    calling this for many different shapes over that handle's lifetime.
  - Adding ANY extra per-thread device memory write inside the kernel
    (tried as a debugging aid, to dump internal state) -- this is NOT
    something we can ship; it doesn't explain the mechanism, and shipping
    dead instrumentation code as a "fix" would be its own kind of dishonest
    patch.

A parallel attempt to route around this entirely by forcing MIOpen's older,
non-CK "static" reduction implementation (MIOPEN_DEBUG_DYNAMIC_REDUCTION=0)
was abandoned: that path has its own independent, pre-existing compile-time
template bug (an Array<int,N> size mismatch inside
CalculateLowerIndexDiff/to_array in the old composable_kernel template
library) and does not currently build at all for this shape family. Fixing
that would be its own open-ended investigation into unmaintained template
metaprogramming, with no guarantee the result is even correct once it
compiles -- rejected as a bigger, riskier, and no more principled rabbit
hole than this one.

The true root cause remains unidentified. It is very likely something at
or below the ROCm driver/runtime level (GPU code-object loading/placement
across multiple JIT-compiled kernels sharing a process, on a codebase and
architecture generation -- gfx803/GCN3 -- long past upstream support) that
is not visible to or fixable from MIOpen's own source.

WHAT
----
Call handle.ClearKernels(algorithm, network_config) and
handle.ClearProgram(program_name, params) -- both existing public Handle
methods, already used elsewhere in MIOpen for cache invalidation -- for
every kernel this function just dispatched, immediately after dispatching
it. Scoped narrowly: only miopenReduceTensor's own dynamic-CK kernel cache
entries are evicted, nothing else in MIOpen's handle-wide kernel cache is
touched, so this cannot affect conv/gemm/or any other operation's cached
kernels.

A handle.Finish() is required immediately before every eviction call. The
first version of this patch omitted it and shipped a NEW, worse bug:
ClearProgram can release the underlying GPU code object, and without
waiting for the just-launched kernel to actually finish first, eviction
raced the kernel's own asynchronous execution. Caught by ORT's real test
suite (onnxruntime_test_all --gtest_filter=*ReductionOpTest*), not by the
standalone MIOpen-only repro -- the repro never interleaves ReduceSum with
other reduction-family ops (ArgMax, ReduceMax, etc.) on the same handle the
way a real ORT process does, and evidently that interleaving matters for
hitting this race. Symptom was ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large returning small
integer sequences (1, 2, 3, 4...) instead of sums -- consistent with the
GPU executing from unloaded/reused code memory, not a numerically-off
result. Lesson for whoever touches this again: the standalone repro is
necessary but not sufficient for validating changes here -- always also
run the real ORT test suite (see the verification note below) before
trusting a build.

COST: every miopenReduceTensor() call using the dynamic-CK path now pays a
full JIT recompile + GPU reload instead of reusing a cached kernel binary,
even when called repeatedly with the exact same shape. This is a real,
measurable per-call performance regression for this one operation --
accepted here because a silently wrong reduction is worse than a slow
correct one, and reduction ops are not typically the hot path relative to
conv/gemm in the model classes this repo cares about. If reduction
performance turns out to matter for a real workload, this patch should be
the first thing revisited, not tuned around.

Applies to: MIOpen @ rocm-6.4.4, gfx803 only -- not upstreamed, this
platform is abandoned; see reduce-kernel-cache-eviction.sh for how this
patch is applied and verified at build time.


FOR WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP TO ROOT-CAUSE PROPERLY
-------------------------------------------------
The real defect is still unknown. Everything mechanistically "obvious" has
been ruled out (see the ruled-out list above). What's confirmed, as a
starting point for going deeper than this investigation had tools for:

  - The failure is deterministic given a fixed handle + fixed call
    sequence, but changes with call order/history -- it is a function of
    which OTHER dynamic-CK reduction kernels have been compiled and cached
    on this SAME Handle before the failing call, not a property of the
    failing shape alone. m=8,9,11,13 at n=128 all share the identical
    compiled kernel (same reduceImpl/tunable/padding bucket) yet only
    m=13, the last of that group dispatched in the default shape-sweep
    order, produced a wrong result -- m=9 and m=11 (also needing padding)
    were fine.
  - Failing output columns are NOT scattered randomly across the output --
    for the m=13,n=128 case a whole valgrind run reproduced the exact
    failure as a single CONTIGUOUS run of wrong columns (j=32..41 out of
    128), which smells like a specific wavefront/SIMD-lane-group boundary,
    not generic memory corruption.
  - What's never been directly observed: an actual wrong byte inside the
    workspace descriptor or the compiled kernel's machine code for a
    failing call. All per-thread/device-side debug instrumentation tried
    (buffer-writes stashing decoded descriptor fields, a sentinel proving
    the prepare kernel's writes are visible to the main kernel) itself
    masks the bug by the mere act of adding an extra memory operation to
    the kernel -- so every attempt to look directly at "what's wrong"
    perturbed the system enough to hide it. This includes at least one
    wasted round where the WRONG source file was instrumented (the
    "_all_dims" wrapper instead of the actually-live "_partial_dims" one
    for this repro's shape family -- confirmed via HIP API tracing,
    AMD_LOG_LEVEL=4, showing the true kernel argument count) before the
    masking-by-any-instrumentation pattern was confirmed even against the
    correct file.
  - Tools not yet tried, roughly in order of likely payoff for cheap
    incremental progress:
      1. Compare the actual compiled ISA (disassemble via `roc-obj-ls` /
         `roc-obj` or similar against the code object MIOpen loads) for the
         SAME kernel_name+params compiled fresh (isolated) vs compiled as
         the Nth kernel in a long sweep, to check whether the generated
         machine code genuinely differs byte-for-byte -- if it does, this
         is a COMGR/LLVM codegen nondeterminism or state-leakage bug
         between separate in-process JIT compiles, not a runtime defect.
      2. If (1) shows identical machine code every time, the defect is at
         GPU code-object LOADING/placement (HSA runtime / kernel driver),
         not compilation -- would need HSA-level tracing (rocprofiler's
         HSA API trace, not just the HIP-level trace already used here) to
         see actual GPU-visible code-object addresses across a session and
         check for overlap between DIFFERENT loaded modules.
      3. rocgdb was not usable in the last environment this was attempted
         in (older ROCm + container combo); this may be worth retrying in
         a bare-metal or newer-ROCm-tooling environment, or with a
         from-source rocgdb build, since a real hardware breakpoint on the
         main kernel with register/memory inspection at the exact point of
         divergence would settle this quickly if it can be made to work.
      4. The static (non-CK) reduction path's own independent compile bug
         (Array<int,N> size mismatch in
         CalculateLowerIndexDiff/to_array, see the abandoned-detour note
         above) is a separate, self-contained yes/no question that doesn't
         require understanding the dynamic-path bug at all -- fixing it
         would at minimum unblock testing whether static reduction is
         actually correct for these shapes, which would make "route
         dynamic-CK's broken cases to static reduction" (the clean,
         Winograd-precedent-style fix this patch deliberately avoided
         because of that compile error) viable.

